From fdf9f05b15cf9f02c900c564686077d8fc30e13f Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: dfattal <dfattal@gmail.com>
Date: Wed, 19 Aug 2026 00:46:56 -0700
Subject: [PATCH] inline-3d(Phase 2): make the flag composite the split
MIME-Version: 1.0
Content-Type: text/plain; charset=UTF-8
Content-Transfer-Encoding: 8bit

Patch 0063 built the plane split and connected none of it. This patch connects
it: with --inline-3d-occlusion on, page content that sits OVER a woven inline-3D
tile is genuinely lifted off the page and composited above the weave instead of
burying it. With the flag off — still the default — nothing here runs and the
frame is the Phase-1 frame byte for byte, so both paths ship in ONE binary and
the hardware A/B is a relaunch, not a rebuild.

Four changes make the flag load-bearing, in the order the pixels flow:

  * SkiaRenderer's difference clip goes live: inline3d_difference_clip_enabled_
    is now IsInline3dOcclusionEnabled() instead of a hardcoded false. The page
    draw is punched out of the tile rects the over-plane has taken over, and the
    batch flush on a clip transition (0063's, deliberately gated on the clip
    being applied) becomes load-bearing at the same moment.
  * WeaveCompositedSurface phase (B) composites the plane: after the whole-window
    woven kSrcOver draw-back, the published over-plane is drawn kSrcOver over the
    whole window (NEAREST, kStrict, inside the existing FlushSurface/submit
    bracket, following the woven draw's resource/semaphore idiom exactly). Risk
    guard R7 is honoured as the field's header demanded: size != window size
    skips with a throttled log rather than sampling a stale-sized plane. Mirrored
    in the macOS phase-B branch, after its per-rect kSrc draw-back, drawn LAST so
    it wins.
  * The v4 DP-composited overlay atlas is stood down (all three blocks: Windows,
    the mac composited-surface path, MaybeWeaveOverlaysMac) and display.cc stops
    suppressing the exclusion-overlay resources. Those two are the Phase-1
    occlusion mechanism, which can only lift content that DECLARED itself; the
    split lifts whatever the draw order says is over a tile. Running both would
    composite the declared overlays twice, and suppressing them while the split
    runs would drop them entirely (0063 excludes already-suppressed resources
    from the over set). Nothing is deleted — dormant, so the A/B stays one
    binary; deletion is 0065.
  * The GPU stage reads the switch itself: inline3d_occlusion_enabled_, cached in
    the SkiaOutputSurfaceImplOnGpu constructor the same way gpu_main.cc reads
    --enable-inline-3d / --inline-3d-sync-weave. ONE switch, two readers — the
    compositor thread punches the page, the GPU thread repairs it, and a split
    verdict would be a hole.

The per-pixel contract inside a tile rect, now exact rather than approximate:
output holds page + under only (the clip removed the over content BEFORE phase
(A) snapshots it, so patch 0050's pre-blend backdrop is the true backdrop, and
that comment now says so); the woven draw covers the rect opaquely (0050 forces
alpha = 1 and the backdrop it pre-blended IS output[tile_rect]); the over-plane
then composites 2D-over, per pixel, with the page's own alpha. Outside the tile
rects the plane is transparent by construction. R9 restated where it lives: the
difference clip is the ONLY thing keeping translucent above-content out of both
the backdrop and the over-plane. Never weaken it. Partial swap needs no change —
the composite is whole-window but reads a surface whose undamaged regions carry
identical pixels, the same contract the woven texture already relies on.

Diagnostics: one throttled marker, "[DisplayXR] inline-3D occlusion composite:",
carrying the over-plane size and drawn/skipped, for the hardware A/B.

The A/B, same binary: --inline-3d-occlusion vs default (or the explicit
--disable-inline-3d-occlusion) must show (1) an UNDECLARED 2D element over a
tile rendering as crisp 2D over 3D with ZERO declarations — the whole point;
(2) a translucent scrim over a tile blending correctly, with no
double-darkening; (3) scroll-glued overlays staying glued (the split is computed
at draw time from the live quad list, so there is no main-thread rect to lag);
and (4) the Phase-1 exclusion corpus unchanged-or-better.

Co-Authored-By: Claude Fable 5 <noreply@anthropic.com>
